===Preposition===
{{en-prep}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} [[behind|Behind]]; toward the [[stern]] [[relative]] to some other [[object]] or [[position]]; [[aft]] of. {{defdate|First attested around the late 15<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#: {{syn|en|abaft [[of]]}}
#: ''The captain stood '''abaft''' the [[wheelhouse]].''
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1620|translator={{w|Thomas Shelton (translator)|Thomas Shelton}}|title=The Second Part of {{w|Don Quixote|the History of the Valorous and Witty Knight-Errant, Don Quixote of the Mancha}}|author=w:Miguel de Cervantes|location=London|publisher=Edward Blount|chapter=63|page=432|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A18335.0001.001
|passage={{...}}  two drunken Turkes, that were in the Frigot with twelue others, discharged two [[caliver|Calieuers]], with which they killed two Souldiours, that stood '''abaft''' our Gally.}}
#* '''1773''', {{w|James Cook}}, ''An Account of a Voyage Around the World,'' Book{{nbsp}}3, Chapter{{nbsp}}5, in {{w|John Hawkesworth (book editor)|John Hawkesworth}} (ed.), ''An Account of the Voyages Undertaken by the Order of His Present Majesty: for Making Discoveries in the Southern Hemisphere,'' London: W. Strahan and T. Cadell, Volume{{nbsp}}3, p.{{nbsp}}558,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004846500.0001.003]</sup>
#*: {{...}} we could hear the water rush in a little '''abaft''' the foremast, about three feet from the keel: this determined me to clear the hold intirely.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1869|author=w:Mark Twain|title=w:The Innocents Abroad|location=Hartford|publisher=The American Publishing Company|chapter=3|page=35|url=https://archive.org/details/innocentsabroad00twairich/page/35/mode/1up?q=abaft
|passage=“{{...}} Read the sign up there—N<small>O SMOKING '''ABAFT''' THE WHEEL</small>!”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1959|author=w:Robert A. Heinlein|title=w:Starship Troopers|location=New York|publisher=Ace|year_published=2010|chapter=13|page=260|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=rmEIDAAAQBAJ&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage=The bulkhead that separates ladies’ country from the rough characters who shave is not necessarily No. 30 but, by tradition, it is called “bulkhead thirty” in any mixed ship. {{...}} Male officers had a lounge called the cardroom just '''abaft''' thirty.}}

